\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{representation-theory}

\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Complex Spin in dimension four is two copies of #{SL_2}}

\p{Let #{V=W\oplus W'} be a split nondegenerate complex quadratic
space with #{\dim W=2}, and choose polar-dual bases
#{e_1,e_2} and #{e'_1,e'_2}. Put
#{S^+=\bigwedge^{\mathrm{even}}W} and
#{S^-=\bigwedge^{\mathrm{odd}}W}, with ordered bases
#{(1,e_1\wedge e_2)} and #{(e_1,e_2)}. The paired
half-spin action from \ref{fcap-002V} gives an algebra equivalence

##{\Phi:\Cl(Q)^0\longrightarrow
\operatorname{End}(S^+)\times\operatorname{End}(S^-)
\simeq M_2(\mathbb C)\times M_2(\mathbb C)}

\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.SpinPolarizationData.evenCliffordEquivProdEnd}.
The two factors here are the actions on the fixed exterior-parity
summands, not merely two abstract matrix blocks.}

\p{For exterior spinors define

##{\langle s,t\rangle=
[e_1\wedge e_2]\bigl(\operatorname{rev}_{\wedge}(s)\wedge t\bigr),}

where the bracket takes the coefficient of #{e_1\wedge e_2}. On each
displayed half-spin basis its matrix is

##{J=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\-1&0\end{pmatrix},}

and the two parity summands are orthogonal. Each creation operator and
each polar-dual contraction operator is self-adjoint for this pairing,
so Clifford reversal becomes the symplectic
adjoint on each factor. Consequently, if #{\Phi(x)=(A,D)}, then

##{\Phi(\operatorname{rev}(x))
=\bigl(J^{-1}A^{\mathsf T}J,J^{-1}D^{\mathsf T}J\bigr)
=\bigl(\operatorname{adj}(A),\operatorname{adj}(D)\bigr).}

Therefore #{\operatorname{rev}(x)x=1} is equivalent to
#{\det A=\det D=1}.}

\p{We use one carrier-completion observation. In any positive-dimensional
nondegenerate complex quadratic space, suppose an even Clifford unit #{u}
satisfies #{\operatorname{rev}(u)u=1} and is already known to normalize
the vector subspace. Its conjugation action is an isometry, so choose a
vector-generated Lipschitz unit with the same orthogonal action
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.lipschitzToOrthogonal_surjective}.
The quotient of that lift and #{u} satisfies #{\alpha(z)v=vz}; graded
centrality makes it a nonzero scalar
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.exists_eq_algebraMap_of_involute_mul_ι_eq_ι_mul}.
Such a scalar unit is Lipschitz
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.unitsMap_algebraMap_mem_lipschitzGroup}.
Hence #{u} is Lipschitz, even, and reverse-norm one, and the exact carrier
criterion of \ref{fcap-0028} puts it in Spin.}

\p{The forward restriction is thus a group homomorphism

##{\Spin(Q)\longrightarrow SL(S^+)\times SL(S^-).}

Conversely, take #{(A,D)\in SL_2(\mathbb C)\times SL_2(\mathbb C)} and
let #{x=\Phi^{-1}(A,D)}. The adjugate calculation makes #{x} an even
Clifford unit with #{\operatorname{rev}(x)x=1}. It remains to prove that
this algebraic preimage lies in the actual vector-generated Spin group.}

\p{For #{v\in V}, set #{y=xvx^{-1}}. It is odd and reversal-fixed:

##{\operatorname{rev}(y)
=\operatorname{rev}(x^{-1})v\operatorname{rev}(x)=y.}

In an orthogonal-basis Clifford expansion in dimension four, the odd
part has only grades one and three. Reversal has signs #{+1} and #{-1}
on those grades, so #{y\in V}. Moreover #{y^2=Q(v)1}; conjugation hence
induces an isometry of #{(V,Q)}. The carrier-completion observation now
places #{x} in the actual Spin group.}

\p{The restriction and #{(A,D)\mapsto\Phi^{-1}(A,D)} are now inverse
group maps. Hence

##{\Spin_4(\mathbb C)\simeq
SL_2(\mathbb C)\times SL_2(\mathbb C),}

and the two half-spin representations are the two standard
two-dimensional modules.
\citet{Exercise 20.39, p. 311}{fulton2004representation}
state this group-and-module identification.
\citet{Section 6.3.3, Exercise 1, p. 322}{goodman2009symmetry}
independently give the same complex group. The displayed pairing, adjugate
calculation, and carrier argument provide the inverse.}

\p{\citet{Section 3.7.6, p. 83}{meinrenken2013clifford}
identifies the compact real form as #{\Spin(4)=SU(2)\times SU(2)}. Its
quaternion-pair model is \ref{fcap-002K}, while the Lorentzian real form
#{SL_2(\mathbb C)} is \ref{fcap-002L}; neither replaces the split complex
proof above. TauCeti formalizes the paired half-spin algebra and matrix maps
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.SpinPolarizationData.evenCliffordEquivProdMatrix},
but not the group image or actual-carrier inverse proved here.}
